 "standard_code": "A.10E, A.10F",
 "standard_url": "https://tea.texas.gov/about-tea/laws-and-rules/sboe-rules-tac/sboe-tac-currently-in-effect/ch111c.pdf",
 "standard_text": "A.10E factor, if possible, trinomials with real factors in the form ax^2 + bx + c, including perfect square trinomials of degree two; A.10F decide if a binomial can be written as the difference of two squares and, if possible, use the structure of a difference of two squares to rewrite the binomial",
 "meta_desc": "Free Algebra I factoring worksheets for Texas TEKS A.10E and A.10F: GCF, x² + bx + c, ax² + bx + c, difference of squares, perfect square trinomials and applications. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "GCF first, then find two numbers.",
 "key_sub": "x² − 2x − 15 = (x − 5)(x + 3)",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>Always take out the <b>greatest common factor</b> first.</li><li><i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>bx</i> + <i>c</i>: find two numbers that multiply to <i>c</i> and add to <i>b</i>.</li><li><b>Difference of squares</b>: <i>a</i><sup>2</sup> − <i>b</i><sup>2</sup> = (<i>a</i> + <i>b</i>)(<i>a</i> − <i>b</i>). A <b>sum</b> of squares does not factor.</li><li><b>Perfect square</b>: <i>a</i><sup>2</sup> + 2<i>ab</i> + <i>b</i><sup>2</sup> = (<i>a</i> + <i>b</i>)<sup>2</sup>.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'><i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 2<i>x</i> − 15</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>−5 × 3 = −15</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>−5 + 3 = −2</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: (<i>x</i> − 5)(<i>x</i> + 3)</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Right numbers, wrong signs</td><td>Check the middle term by multiplying.</td></tr><tr><td>Factoring <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + 9</td><td>A sum of squares does not factor.</td></tr><tr><td>Forgetting the GCF</td><td>Take it out first.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
